{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Atarot, etc. the land, etc.</b> It is necessary to know: first, why were they required to enumerate all the cities, and was it not sufficient with the general statement they made, ״הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳״ etc., in which all those lands are included? Or they should have said in the manner that the verse mentioned them just before, in two general headings, as it is written ״אֶת אֶרֶץ יַעְזֵר וְאֶת אֶרֶץ גִּלְעָד״ (Numbers 32:1). Second, its saying <b>הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר</b> etc. — were there other lands before them whose names were likewise such that they needed to designate these? And if to say that HaShem struck them — who does not know this?" "<b>Now</b> the intention is along this path: these men were wise to speak in an order that would leave no room to raise objections against their request — the objections that could indeed be raised on the matter. First, since this land belongs to the collective of Israel, what authority do they have to request something belonging to others? Second, how could it enter their minds to dwell securely in a land that was already conquered while the rest of the tribes would enter the danger of the awesome battles prepared for them — and every man of Israel would say: I will speak just as you speak, and I will not put myself in the danger of the peoples! And all of this is because it touches upon the negation of favor from Moses and Israel — apart from what one could rebuke them for, that they choose to dwell outside the Holy Land, the one chosen by HaShem." "<b>Therefore</b> the children of Gad and the children of Reuben were cunning in their first statement, and gave an answer to three detrimental things that mar their request, in what they said: <b>Atarot</b> etc. <b>הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳</b> etc. — against the first objection, that the land belongs to the collective of Israel, there is in this statement an answer to this: that they were not taking the land in a natural manner, to say ״is it not ours?״, but rather HaShem is the one who struck its inhabitants before them, and this is nothing but heavenly property, and it is from the property of the Most High that we come to request." "<b>Also</b> the second objection — that their brothers would enter the danger of wars, etc. — they demolished with this statement <b>אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳</b>, meaning: granted, if the inheritance of the land were in a natural manner, this objection would hold; but since HaShem is the one who strikes the inhabitants of the land before them, what room is there for fear of the danger of wars? And this is your sign: הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳ לִפְנֵיהֶם — and this is a sign for all the land, as it is written in parashat Devarim (Deuteronomy 3:21): ״וְאֶת יְהוֹשֻׁעַ צִוֵּיתִי בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר עֵינֶיךָ הָרֹאוֹת אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם לִשְׁנֵי מַלְכֵי הָאֱמֹרִי כֵּן יַעֲשֶׂה ה׳ לְכָל הַמַּמְלָכוֹת אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עֹבֵר שָׁמָּה״. From this point on there is no room for the objection of fear of battle. And they also intended through this to remove from the suspicion that falls upon their request — that people would say they did so only out of fear of the war for the land." "<b>Moreover,</b> the objection of rejecting the Holy Land — they gave an answer in the statement <b>אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳</b>, meaning: according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of the Laws of Terumot, and this is his language: ״Eretz Yisrael mentioned everywhere is land that a king of Israel or a prophet conquered with the knowledge of the majority of Israel; but an individual of Israel or a tribe that went and conquered for themselves a place, even from the land that was given to Abraham, is not called Eretz Yisrael,״ end quote. And he wrote further there regarding the status of lands that David conquered, and this is his language: ״And why did they decline from the sanctity of Eretz Yisrael? Because he conquered them before all of Eretz Yisrael was conquered, in which some of the seven nations remained,״ end quote. And the essence of this law is brought in the Sifrei (end of parashat Eikev), who derived the law of other lands from Eretz Yisrael from the verse (Deuteronomy 11:24) ״כָּל מָקוֹם אֲשֶׁר תִּדְרֹךְ כַּף רַגְלְכֶם בּוֹ לָכֶם יִהְיֶה״ — to teach about places conquered outside Eretz Yisrael, from the desert and the Lebanon etc. to the western sea etc. And they derived that Eretz Yisrael must be conquered first, since it is written before the statement ״כָּל מָקוֹם״ — ״וִירִשְׁתֶּם גּוֹיִם גְּדוֹלִים״ etc., so that Eretz Yisrael not remain defiled by idolatry while you are conquering outside the land etc., end quote. These words declare that without this reason of precedence, wherever the majority of Israel conquered it, the law of Eretz Yisrael applies to it for everything. And this is the statement of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben: <b>הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳ לִפְנֵי עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — meaning, this land has the sanctity of the Land, for the reason that they conquered it before the congregation of Israel, and the conquest of the many gives it the law of Eretz Yisrael for all its matters." "<b>And as for the claim</b> that the Sifre was precise in saying that conquest outside the Land prior to the conquest of the Land does not have the status of the Land of Israel — and if so, the land of Sihon and Og was [conquered] before the conquest of the Land [and thus should not count] — therefore they preempted this claim with the statement <b>which HaShem struck</b>, meaning, this conquest is extraordinary because it came about by the word of HaShem. For He said to Moses concerning the land of Sihon: ״הֵחֵל רָשׁ אֶת אַרְצוֹ״ as stated in parashat Devarim (Deuteronomy 2:31), and also concerning the land of Og (Deuteronomy 3:2) HaShem said there: ״רָאִיתָה אֶת עוֹג מֶלֶךְ הַבָּשָׁן כִּי תוֹרָשׁ אֹתוֹ וְאֶת כָּל עַמּוֹ עָשִׂיתָ לוֹ״ — and furthermore (Deuteronomy 3:2): ״בְּיָדְךָ נָתַתִּי אוֹתוֹ וְאֶת אַרְצוֹ וְאֶת כָּל עַמּוֹ וְעָשִׂיתָ לּוֹ״ etc. From this point on, HaShem consented to the priority. And there is also another reason in the matter: they did not have before them two conquests, one of the Land and one outside the Land, such that they would have prioritized that of outside the Land in the manner that David did, who took Aram Tzovah before he dispossessed the Jebusite as stated there — to say, why did you refrain from conquering the Land of Israel first? On the contrary, the conquest of the land of Sihon and Og was necessary for the conquest of the Land, so that they could pass through there to conquer it. And they had already sent him words of peace to give them passage through his land, but he refused. From this point on there is no grievance against their request from HaShem and from Israel." "<b>And with this</b> there is a sound reason why they needed to mention the specific names of the lands. For according to what we explained in the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳״ — to say that they came to ask only for the property of the Most High — they said that all the specific districts, every one of them, they did not conquer by natural means but rather HaShem struck them before them. And also, inasmuch as they came to secure them with the presumption of the sanctity of the Land, they were precise to mention all the names, to say that each one of them partakes of the sanctity of the Land, and any other district besides these is not included. And the conclusion that follows from this is that any city across the Jordan besides these does not have for them the status of the Land of Israel, because it is not a conquest by the many." "And their saying <b>it is a land for livestock and your servants have livestock</b> — they intended to remove yet another objection, namely: after we concede all the arguments they made, still any tribe of Israel could come and make the very same arguments to take the land, for those very arguments are valid for every tribe of Israel. Therefore they said: no other tribe needs it as they do, because it is a land for livestock, and they have abundant livestock."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:05.783516545Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}